---
/**
 * ReasoningTrace: homepage section 5, "Run the real engine".
 *
 * DESIGN: R3 "Five-step rail", Figma frame 65:2 on page
 * `Section — Reasoning trace` (63:2). Owner decision, 2026-08-02, recorded in
 * specs/HOMEPAGE-DECISION-TABLE.md. Eight variants were rendered; this is the
 * one taken. It is not the one I recommended.
 *
 * THE REFUSAL IS THE SECTION. Four figures are claimed on one live award
 * question. Three trace back to a commitment somebody recorded. The fourth,
 * `local supply chain spend`, has nothing behind it, so it is dropped and the
 * drop is drawn, hung under Ground, in the refusal colour, named. A version of
 * this section that keeps the five-step rail and loses the dropped figure has
 * kept the picture and thrown away the argument. This is the one element that
 * must survive every future edit, including edits made for space.
 *
 * WHY THAT MATTERS COMMERCIALLY. This is the section that defines the product
 * against competitors selling success-rate uplift. It has to read as an audit
 * of one answer, not as a feature list, because the claim is not "we write
 * better bids". It is "every figure we ship traces to something you recorded,
 * and the ones that do not, we refuse". Refusing to claim a win rate is the
 * position; the drop is what makes it visible in one glance.
 *
 * NUMBERED, AND THAT IS EARNED. 01-05 are used here where BothSides refuses
 * them, because these genuinely are ordered: you cannot check a figure you have
 * not computed, or cite a commitment you have not grounded. In BothSides the
 * three stations are the same contract at three moments, so numbering them
 * would assert a sequence that is not there.
 *
 * THE 40/30/20 WEIGHTINGS KEEP THEIR HEDGE. A source comment once called them
 * "the published weighting breakdown" while naming no authority, and the eyebrow
 * above them says "Run the real engine", so specific figures here would read as
 * a real authority's published criteria. The standing rule has two options and
 * no third: a figure is cited to a named source, or it is visibly marked as an
 * example. These are marked, in the shortest form that still marks them, and the
 * word "Illustrative" is the part that may never be cut. Note this is the ONE
 * narrow exception to "no hedging labels on product graphics". Do not let it
 * breed.
 *
 * ── WHAT THIS PASS CUT, AND WHERE IT WENT ───────────────────────────────────
 *
 * Owner review, 2026-08-02, second pass: "Same issues with ... 'Run the real
 * engine': we are giving so much textual information", and the verdict on the
 * page as a whole, that it reads as a research site rather than a product site.
 * Four things went, and the test was: cover the words, does the rail plus the
 * refusal still make the point?
 *
 *   the five step captions   Yes. Retrieve, Ground, Compute, Check, Cite are
 *                            five verbs in order with a refusal hanging off the
 *                            second. The captions ("arithmetic runs in code" and
 *                            so on) explained in prose what the step name and
 *                            the drop box already show. THE GEOMETRY IS
 *                            UNTOUCHED: five columns, five dots, one rail, the
 *                            drop still hung under Ground. Only a text node per
 *                            step is gone, which is a word cut, not a redesign
 *                            of R3. It also leaves the rail free for the motion
 *                            work now in progress.
 *   the tally labels         Shortened, not removed. "Figures claimed" became
 *                            "Claimed" and so on; the numbers and the refusal
 *                            colour are the content.
 *   the National TOMs foot   Moved to /sources, in fuller form: the framework
 *                            gets its own entry, the "aligned to conventions
 *                            rather than a full implementation" wording is kept
 *                            verbatim, and the £27,000 in the sum below is
 *                            explicitly listed there as an ILLUSTRATIVE
 *                            placeholder and NOT a National TOMs proxy value.
 *                            That is stronger than the sentence it replaced.
 *   "a named person signs
 *    the answer off"         Product positioning, and it is made at length on
 *                            /crowmark, which this section links.
 *
 * THE ONE THING THAT MAY NEVER BE CUT FOR SPACE is the dropped figure. A version
 * of this section with a shorter rail and no refusal has kept the picture and
 * thrown away the argument.
 *
 * ── AND THE EXAMPLE IS NO LONGER PUBLIC-SECTOR ONLY (A-104 item 8) ──────────
 *
 * Owner, 2026-08-05, and this is a POSITIONING fix rather than a copy edit. The
 * worked example was a UK local authority social value question, in a section
 * headed "Run the real engine", on a site whose recorded position (OA-25) is
 * that the company is market-neutral and UK public procurement is its PROOF
 * POINT and not its market. The one artefact the homepage shows the engine
 * working on was therefore contradicting the positioning of the whole site.
 *
 * THREE THINGS CHANGED AND NOTHING ELSE DID. The question lost "the borough"
 * and "social value" (see the note on QUESTION). The artefact now names what it
 * is, a private-sector RFP, and says the same run answers a public notice (see
 * PROVENANCE). And the question takes the panel's width instead of a 68ch
 * column inside it, which is the "must be wider" half of the instruction.
 *
 * THE DIAGRAM IS UNTOUCHED, BY INSTRUCTION. The owner's words are that the
 * style is approved and must not change. Five steps, five dots, one rail, the
 * drop hung under Ground, the tally, the sum and the illustrative tag are all
 * exactly as they were. Nothing in this pass moved a pixel of R3.
 *
 * WHAT THE NEW EXAMPLE DOES NOT DO, STATED SO NOBODY HAS TO CHECK. It names no
 * customer, no company and no sector body. It carries no new figure: the only
 * figures in the section remain the tally counts, which are the section's own
 * arithmetic, and £27,000 and £108,000, which /sources lists explicitly as
 * illustrative placeholders and not National TOMs proxy values. It claims no
 * win rate and no probability of award, which is the position this section
 * exists to hold.
 *
 * THE REFUSAL STILL WORKS IN THE WIDENED EXAMPLE, AND THAT WAS THE TEST. "local
 * supply chain spend, no recorded commitment" is a question a corporate buyer
 * asks in a sustainability schedule at least as often as a contracting
 * authority asks it, so the figure that gets dropped is dropped for the same
 * reason in either market. If it had not survived the change of buyer, the
 * example would have been the wrong one.
 *
 * NO CONTENT DEPENDS ON JAVASCRIPT. Every step, every caption and the dropped
 * figure are in the HTML and painted at first render. The only script animates
 * a sum from zero towards the value ALREADY IN THE DOM, parsed from the element
 * rather than restated in the script, so it cannot disagree with the markup and
 * a failure leaves the correct number on screen.
 *
 * THIS SECTION TAUGHT THAT LESSON TWICE. A staged entrance reveal was written
 * here first and failed the sitewide axe gate with colour-contrast on 7 nodes,
 * because the checker sampled while the steps were mid-transition and
 * semi-transparent text meets 4.5:1 against nothing. The contrast failure was
 * the symptom. The fault was making correct content depend on an observer
 * firing, which is the same fault that once left four of nine homepage sections
 * permanently invisible. Motion may refine what is already there. It may never
 * create it.
 */
import Section from '../layout/Section.astro';

interface Step {
  n: string;
  /**
   * ONE WORD, AND IT IS LOAD-BEARING TWICE. Single words cannot wrap, so the
   * rows above the rail are the same height in every column and the rail lands
   * on one line with nothing to measure. And since the captions came off, the
   * name is the entire step: it has to be a verb that says what happened.
   */
  name: string;
  /**
   * ── THE LOG LINE, 2026-08-07 ─────────────────────────────────────────────
   *
   * What this stage DID, in the engine's own terms. Adopted from the Ultra-
   * Premium concept, whose engine section gives every stage a line of detail
   * under its name and reads as an execution log rather than as five labels.
   * That presentation is the half of the concept worth having.
   *
   * ITS CONTENT IS NOT THE CONCEPT'S. That version invents a notice number, two
   * PDF filenames, a citation reference and an "AUDIT HASH", and captions the
   * result "100% Citation Coverage Mapped" over a "Tamper-Proof Audit Record".
   * None of those exist, and inventing evidence is the precise failure this
   * whole section is built to demonstrate the engine refusing. It also puts the
   * refusal at stage 04; in this trace it happens at DROP_AT, stage 02, because
   * GROUNDING is where a figure fails.
   *
   * SO EVERY LINE BELOW IS DRAWN FROM SOMETHING ALREADY ON THIS PAGE. The
   * question, the dropped claim, the worked sum, the tally and the closing
   * sentence about who signs off. Nothing here is a new claim, and the one
   * figure carries the same `illustrative` marking it carries in the sum.
   */
  detail: string;
}

/**
 * The award question. Shortened from 20 words to 11 in the 2026-08-02 pass that
 * cut the captions, and REWRITTEN on 2026-08-05 under A-104 item 8. It is the
 * artefact being audited rather than copy, so it has to keep reading like
 * something a real buyer would ask: a demand for quantified benefit
 * attributable to this contract, not a general invitation to describe good
 * intentions.
 *
 * ── WHY IT CHANGED, AND IT IS POSITIONING RATHER THAN WORDING ──────────────
 *
 * It read: "Quantify the social value this contract will create in the
 * borough." Owner instruction, 2026-08-05: "Run the real engine needs a WIDER
 * example that is not public-sector only", against owner decision OA-25, which
 * is that this company is MARKET-NEUTRAL and UK public procurement is its proof
 * point rather than its market.
 *
 * TWO WORDS WERE DOING THE DAMAGE AND BOTH ARE GONE. "the borough" names a UK
 * local authority and nothing else, so the one worked example on the homepage
 * was set inside the one market the site is at pains not to be limited to.
 * "social value" is a term of art from PPN 002: a reader who knows what it
 * means reads a public tender, and a reader who does not learns that this
 * product is for people who do.
 *
 * WHAT REPLACES THEM ASSERTS NOTHING NEW. "community and environmental benefit"
 * is a demand a corporate buyer makes in the sustainability section of an RFP
 * and a demand a contracting authority makes under a social value question, in
 * the same words, and the site already publishes both halves of that: /blog's
 * private-sector RFP and PQQ guide sets out at length that a corporate buyer
 * scores against its own weighted matrix with no statutory rulebook, and
 * ProofGate says on this same page that the same engine runs a private-sector
 * bid. No customer, no company, no sector body and no figure is named or
 * implied by the sentence.
 *
 * IT IS ALSO STILL ONE SENTENCE AND STILL TEN WORDS, so the panel's geometry,
 * its wrap behaviour and the 2026-08-02 word cut are all untouched.
 */
const QUESTION =
  'Quantify the community and environmental benefit this contract will create.';

/**
 * WHAT DOCUMENT THE QUESTION CAME OUT OF, AND IT IS THE HALF OF ITEM 8 THE
 * QUESTION ITSELF CANNOT CARRY.
 *
 * Neutralising the wording stops the example reading as public-sector-only. It
 * does not make the example read as WIDE, because a question with no market in
 * it has no market in it, and a reader supplies the one they already assume. So
 * the artefact names itself, in the provenance register the site uses for a
 * date, a reference or a stamp.
 *
 * BOTH SENTENCES ARE THINGS THIS SITE ALREADY PUBLISHES. The first identifies
 * the document on screen. The second is the market-neutrality claim itself, and
 * it is deliberately the weaker direction of it: not "we cover everything", but
 * "this same run answers the other kind of buyer", which is exactly what the
 * five steps below do, since not one of them cites a statute.
 *
 * IT IS THE SAME FACT ProofGate STATES EIGHT SECTIONS UP, AND THAT IS RECORDED
 * RATHER THAN GLOSSED. ProofGate makes the claim in the abstract, about the
 * engine, before anything has been shown. This one is a caption on the artefact
 * a reader is looking at, and without it the artefact silently picks a market
 * for them. If a future pass finds the pair repetitive, the question to ask is
 * which of the two is attached to something on screen.
 *
 * NO FIGURE, NO NAMED BUYER, NO CLAIM ABOUT OUTCOMES. The standing rule on this
 * section is that a figure is cited to a named source or visibly marked as an
 * example, and the safest way to satisfy it in a caption is to carry no figure
 * at all.
 */
const PROVENANCE = 'A private-sector RFP. The same run answers a public notice.';

/*
 * THE 40/30/20 AWARD WEIGHTINGS ARE GONE FROM THE HOMEPAGE ENTIRELY, and this
 * is the cleanest thing the 2026-08-02 cut achieved.
 *
 * They were the ONE narrow exception to "no hedging labels on product graphics":
 * three figures no authority publishes, sitting under an eyebrow reading "Run
 * the real engine", kept only because a visible "Illustrative award criteria"
 * caption marked them. That worked, but it cost a caption, three labels and
 * three figures of homepage text to say something the section does not argue.
 * The section argues that an ungrounded figure gets refused. How the answer is
 * weighted is a different subject, and MarketShape already carries the one
 * weighting that is a real published duty, the PPN 002 10% floor.
 *
 * So the exception is not narrowed, it is closed: there is now no illustrative
 * figure anywhere on the homepage except the worked sum below, which /sources
 * lists explicitly as an illustrative placeholder rather than a National TOMs
 * proxy value.
 *
 * DO NOT REINTRODUCE THEM WITHOUT THE CAPTION. If a future edit wants award
 * weightings back on this page, the rule is unchanged and has two options and no
 * third: cite them to a named authority, or mark them visibly as an example.
 */

const STEPS: Step[] = [
  {
    n: '01',
    name: 'Retrieve',
    detail: 'The published question is read with its wording intact.',
  },
  {
    n: '02',
    name: 'Ground',
    detail: 'Every figure is matched to a confirmed commitment. One is not, and is dropped here.',
  },
  {
    n: '03',
    name: 'Compute',
    detail: 'What survives is computed from those commitments, never generated.',
  },
  {
    n: '04',
    name: 'Check',
    detail: 'The draft is counted against what it claimed: four asked for, three traced.',
  },
  {
    n: '05',
    name: 'Cite',
    detail: 'Each surviving figure carries its source, and a named person approves it.',
  },
];

/** The step the refusal hangs from. Grounding is where a figure fails. */
const DROP_AT = '02';

/**
 * The audit result. Read as a tally rather than a claim: three counts that sum
 * to the four figures the draft asked for, so a reader can check the arithmetic
 * of the section itself.
 */
const TALLY = [
  { k: 'Claimed', v: '4' },
  { k: 'Traced', v: '3' },
  { k: 'Refused', v: '1', refused: true },
];

/**
 * ── THE WORKED SUM IS A NUMBER NOW, AND THE STRING IS DERIVED FROM IT ───────
 *
 * THE DEFECT THIS CLOSES, confirmed at HEAD. The count-up at the foot of this
 * file took its target by reading `£108,000` back out of the DOM and stripping
 * every character that is not a digit or a dot. It then formatted the frames
 * with `Intl.NumberFormat`. So the value went OUT through a formatter and came
 * back IN through a parser, and the two had no agreement of any kind: a
 * thousands separator that is a space rather than a comma, a currency symbol
 * that is not one character, a locale whose group separator is a full stop, or
 * simply a hand-edit writing `£108,000 per year` would each have produced a
 * different number, or NaN, and the count would have run to the wrong figure
 * or not at all. Nothing would have failed. The page would have shown a wrong
 * total in the one section whose entire subject is arithmetic you can check.
 *
 * SO THE NUMBER IS THE SOURCE AND EVERY STRING IS COMPUTED FROM IT. The three
 * figures in the sentence below are one multiplication, written once. The
 * template formats them, the element carries the target as data, and the script
 * reads that attribute and formats with the IDENTICAL formatter. There is no
 * parse anywhere, so there is nothing for a separator or a locale to break.
 *
 * IT IS THE SHAPE A COUNT-UP SHOULD TAKE ANYWHERE ON THIS SITE: the figure is
 * data, the presentation is a function of it, and the script and the template
 * call the same function rather than agreeing to write the same thing.
 *
 * £27,000 KEEPS ITS HEDGE AND ITS PROVENANCE. It is not a published figure from
 * a named authority and specifically NOT a National TOMs proxy value, which
 * /sources says in as many words. Nothing about that changes by naming it.
 */
const SUM_JOBS = 4;
const SUM_PER_JOB = 27000;
const SUM_TOTAL = SUM_JOBS * SUM_PER_JOB;
const GBP = new Intl.NumberFormat('en-GB', {
  style: 'currency',
  currency: 'GBP',
  maximumFractionDigits: 0,
});
---

{/*
  ── THE CORE PRINCIPLE, MIXED INTO THIS SECTION 2026-08-04 ──────────────────

  Owner instruction: section 7 of the homepage is this section, the live one,
  MIXED with "The core principle" from concepts/homepage-v2.html. The concept
  gives that principle its own full-width band: a refusal chip, the heading "If
  a figure cannot be traced, the engine refuses it.", a lede about what a
  generative tool does instead, a four-row ledger of three traced claims and one
  refused, and a closing line about who signs off.

  WHAT MOVED ACROSS IS THE ARGUMENT. WHAT DID NOT IS THE LEDGER, AND THAT IS THE
  WHOLE REASON THIS IS A MIX RATHER THAN A SECOND SECTION. The concept's ledger
  is four rows: three statutory figures stamped TRACED, and "local supply chain
  spend" stamped REFUSED. Every one of those four is already on this page. The
  three statutory rows are the figures of the statutory section two sections
  above, with their citations. That section carried four of them until
  2026-08-05 and carries three now; the count is deliberately not restated here,
  because what this paragraph turns on is that the claims are already on the
  page and not on how many there are. The refused row is the figure this
  section's own rail drops at stage 02, by name, with its reason. Rendering the
  ledger here would print the page's own contents back at the reader in a third
  form, which is exactly the "prose transcript of a graphic" the 2026-08-02 pass
  cut from this section.

  So the principle arrives as the two things the drawing genuinely cannot say:
  the statement of what the engine does instead of inventing, above; and who is
  left holding the decision, below. The drawing performs the refusal in between.

  WHAT THIS REPLACES. The block here used to read "NO STANDFIRST. It read 'Four
  figures claimed. Three trace back. The fourth never reaches the draft.' Every
  one of those three counts is printed in the result box below as a tally." That
  objection was correct and it does not reach the sentences below, none of which
  restates a count, a step or a figure.
*/}
<Section
  eyebrow="Run the real engine"
  title="Watch it refuse a figure."
  standfirst="If a figure cannot be traced, the engine refuses it. A generative tool asked for a number it does not have will produce a plausible one. CrowAgent does the opposite, and it does it visibly."
  id="reasoning"
>
  {/*
    `data-lit` at threshold 0.15, per `147:3`: this section is tall, so 15% of
    it is most of a screen. `data-rt` is the count-up's own hook and is
    unrelated; the two triggers stay separate because the count-up already
    ships, already has its own played-once flag, and must not be re-plumbed
    through a new mechanism in the same pass that adds one.
  */}
  <div class="rt" data-spot="off" data-rt data-lit data-lit-threshold="0.15">
    {/*
      THE AMBIENT RIG, ADDED 2026-08-02 AND CUT TO ONE FIELD 2026-08-05. It is
      a symmetry fix rather than a decoration: six of the seven animated
      homepage sections carried screen-blended fields behind them and this one
      carried none, which is why
      it read as the flattest block on the page and why it had no ambient motion
      at all below 860px, where its rail turns vertical and its travelling light
      has almost nothing to travel. Same recipe as the other five, at the same
      scale, with the same one-gutter bleed and the same radial mask on all four
      edges: see MarketShape.astro for the arithmetic behind both.

      `data-field` opts it into the shared ambient layer in styles/tokens.css,
      which is what makes the drift and the entrance identical to every other
      section rather than six more numbers in this file.
    */}
    <div class="rt__amb" data-field aria-hidden="true">
      <b class="rt__amb-a"></b>
      {/* The cursor light and the grain, inside the wrapper so both stay behind
          every word and inherit its mask. <i>, so neither joins the drift. */}
      <i class="sv-spot"></i>
      <i class="sv-grain"></i>
    </div>

    {/*
      "WORKED EXAMPLE", NOT "QUESTION 4.2", AND THE CHANGE IS AN ACCURACY FIX
      RATHER THAN A REWORD. Cutting the 40/30/20 criteria took the visible
      "Illustrative award criteria" caption off the page with them, and that
      caption was also the only thing on the homepage marking the whole scenario
      as hypothetical. Under an eyebrow reading "Run the real engine", an
      unmarked "Question 4.2" and an unmarked £27,000 read as a real authority's
      real tender. Two words at the head of the section body now mark the
      scenario, and the sum below carries its own tag. Same word count as the
      label it replaced, so the fix cost nothing but the invented document
      number.
    */}
    <div class="rt__brief sv-raised">
      <p class="eyebrow rt__brief-label">Worked example</p>
      <p class="rt__q">{QUESTION}</p>
      {/*
        THE PROVENANCE SITS UNDER THE QUESTION, NOT ABOVE IT, AND THE ORDER IS
        THE ARGUMENT. Above the question it would be a filing label and a reader
        would meet the market before the demand. Under it, it answers the
        question a reader has just formed ("whose tender is this?"), which is
        the question the old wording answered by accident and wrongly.

        `.meta` rather than a plain paragraph, and that is the register this
        site reserves for provenance: a date, a reference, a stamp, set in
        uppercase mono at --t-micro. Naming what a document is, is exactly that,
        and it is why this line may be short enough to shout without reading as
        shouting. The sign-off at the foot of this section takes the base `p`
        instead, for the opposite reason, recorded there.
      */}
      <p class="meta rt__brief-src">{PROVENANCE}</p>
    </div>

    {/*
      THE RAIL. Five steps on one line. The dot and the line are decoration:
      remove every pixel of them and the ordered list still reads correctly in
      order, which is the test a diagram has to pass before it ships.
    */}
    {/*
      THE RAIL STANDS ON A RAISED PANEL, WHICH IS WHAT FIGMA `214:92` DRAWS AND
      WHAT THE MEASUREMENT ASKED FOR. specs/ULTRA-PREMIUM-GAP.md measured this
      section at 0.01 gradient coverage against 0.32 to 0.64 elsewhere on the
      page: it had no atmosphere at all, which was an omission rather than a
      decision anybody took. The ambient rig fixed half of that; this fixes the
      other half by giving the five steps a surface to stand on.
    */}
    {/*
      THE PANEL IS A <figure> AND THE STEPS ARE STILL AN <ol>.

      A figure, because this is a drawing: five dots on a rail with the step
      number and name set above each dot, and a refusal hung under Ground. Those
      are labels pinned to geometry, so asking them to sit on the panel's centre
      line would take each one off the dot it belongs to. scripts/check-render.js
      excludes <figure> for that reason.

      An <ol>, because the five steps are a sequence and the comment above says
      so: remove every pixel of the dot and the line and the ordered list still
      reads correctly in order. A <figure> element cannot carry that, so the
      panel wraps the list rather than replacing it.
    */}
    <figure class="rt__railpanel sv-raised">
    <ol class="rt__rail">
      {
        STEPS.map((s) => (
          <li class="rt__step">
            {/*
              THE STEP COUNTER AT 64px, ON THE BETTER STACK RECIPE. It rendered
              at 11px (smaller than the body copy) under a section whose whole
              subject is an ordered sequence, so the one element that carried
              the order was the quietest thing in the column. `sv-numeral` is
              defined once in styles/surfaces.css with a solid full-contrast
              fallback first. See --t-counter in tokens.css.

              STILL aria-hidden, and that is unchanged and load-bearing: the
              order is already in the <ol> and in the visually-hidden "Step 01:"
              prefix on the heading below. Making the counter bigger must not
              make it a second, competing announcement.
            */}
            <p class="meta rt__n sv-numeral" aria-hidden="true">{s.n}</p>
            <h3 class="rt__name">
              <span class="visually-hidden">Step {s.n}: </span>
              {s.name}
            </h3>

            {/* The log line. Not aria-hidden: it is the only place the page
                says what each stage actually does, so a screen reader gets it
                with the name it belongs to. */}
            <p class="rt__detail">{s.detail}</p>
            <span class="rt__railcell" aria-hidden="true">
              {/*
                The key light for this step. Per-cell, like the both-sides
                rail and for the same reason: the rail's y falls out of a grid
                nothing can measure in CSS. Each cell runs 300ms. The light
                crosses to the dot, then PAUSES 180ms on it before moving on,
                which is what makes five steps read as five decisions rather
                than as one sweep.
              */}
              <span class="rt__track">
                <span class="rt__beam" />
              </span>
              <span class="rt__dot" />
            </span>

            {s.n === DROP_AT && (
              <div class="rt__drop">
                <span class="rt__drop-tick" aria-hidden="true" />
                <p class="rt__drop-box">
                  {/* Drawn, not typed. JetBrains Mono has no ballot-X glyph and
                      a missing glyph in this face renders as a stray character,
                      which is exactly what happened to an arrow on the section
                      above. An inline SVG needs no font at all. */}
                  <svg class="rt__x" viewBox="0 0 12 12" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
                    <path
                      d="M2.5 2.5 L9.5 9.5 M9.5 2.5 L2.5 9.5"
                      stroke="currentColor"
                      stroke-width="1.6"
                      stroke-linecap="round"
                    />
                    {/*
                      A SECOND, BRIGHTER COPY OF THIS PATH USED TO SIT HERE and
                      draw itself in over the top in two 160ms strokes. It was
                      removed on 2026-08-03 with its animation (see the note in
                      the stylesheet below), because it existed for nothing else,
                      and an SVG path dashed permanently out of view is markup
                      that renders nothing forever. The cross above was always
                      the refusal mark and is unchanged.
                    */}
                  </svg>
                  <span class="rt__drop-name">local supply chain spend</span>
                  <span class="rt__drop-why">no recorded commitment</span>
                  <span class="chip rt__drop-tag">dropped here</span>
                </p>
              </div>
            )}
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ol>

    {/*
      ── THE SCRUBBER, 2026-08-31 ────────────────────────────────────────────

      Owner: let a visitor step through the five stages, with the verification
      marks landing as they go and the refusal firing where it happens.

      A RANGE INPUT, AND IT IS THE LITERAL INSTRUMENT RATHER THAN A DRAWN ONE.
      It is the one native control that means "a position in a sequence", and
      it arrives with the whole keyboard contract already correct: arrows step,
      Home and End go to the ends, Page keys jump, and the value is announced.
      A row of five buttons would have been five more targets to size, a roving
      tabindex to write, and a second answer to a question `role="tab"` on this
      page already answers.

      IT SHIPS `hidden` AND THE SCRIPT REVEALS IT, which is the identical
      contract `.rt__toggle` below and `.wt__pause` and `.tabsw-auto__pause`
      elsewhere on this page are under. With no script there is no scrubber,
      rather than a slider that moves and changes nothing.

      AND THE DIAGRAM IS UNTOUCHED UNTIL A READER TOUCHES IT. Owner instruction,
      2026-08-05, is that this drawing is approved and must not change: five
      steps, five dots, one rail, the drop hung under Ground. So no step carries
      a state attribute until the first `input` event, and every rule keyed on
      one therefore matches nothing on a page nobody has scrubbed. The resting
      section is byte-for-byte the section that shipped.

      NOTHING IT DOES REDUCES CONTRAST, AND THAT IS NOT A PREFERENCE. This
      section has already failed the axe gate once on colour-contrast across
      seven nodes, because a staged reveal left text semi-transparent while the
      checker sampled it. The obvious scrubber dims the steps ahead of the
      playhead and would walk straight back into it. So every state below only
      ever RAISES contrast: the current step's log line moves from the quietest
      ink to the next one up, and the steps ahead are left exactly as they are.
    */}
    <figcaption class="rt__scrub" data-rt-scrub hidden>
      <label class="meta rt__scrub-label" for="rt-scrub">Step through the trace</label>
      <input
        class="rt__scrub-range"
        id="rt-scrub"
        type="range"
        min="1"
        max={STEPS.length}
        value={STEPS.length}
        step="1"
        data-rt-range
      />
    </figcaption>
    </figure>

    {/* The result of the audit, and the one figure that survived to be shown.

        A <figure>, and the word in that sentence was already doing the work.
        This is the third panel of one drawing: the ledger of what was claimed
        against what survived, with the refused row struck in orchid, and the
        arithmetic underneath it. Its rows are a two-column tally read across,
        so their alignment is their meaning. */}
    <figure class="rt__result sv-raised">
      <dl class="rt__tally">
        {
          TALLY.map((t) => (
            <div class:list={['rt__tally-row', { 'rt__tally-row--refused': t.refused }]}>
              <dt>{t.k}</dt>
              <dd>{t.v}</dd>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </dl>

      {/* "per year" stays. Without it the arithmetic is ambiguous, and an
          ambiguous sum in the section about traceable arithmetic would be the
          one cut that costs more than the words it saves. */}
      <p class="rt__sum">
        <span>{SUM_JOBS} jobs × {GBP.format(SUM_PER_JOB)} per year =</span>{' '}
        {/* THE TARGET TRAVELS AS DATA, NOT AS TEXT, and that is the whole of
            the fix recorded beside SUM_TOTAL. aria-live is off: a number
            ticking many times a second must never be announced. */}
        <b data-rt-sum data-rt-total={SUM_TOTAL} aria-live="off">{GBP.format(SUM_TOTAL)}</b>{' '}
        {/*
          THE ONE-WORD HEDGE, AND IT MAY NEVER BE CUT FOR SPACE. £27,000 is not
          a published figure from a named authority and is specifically NOT a
          National TOMs proxy value; /sources says so in as many words. The
          standing rule has two options and no third: cite a figure to a named
          source, or mark it visibly as an example. This is the mark, and it is
          in the same line as the figure rather than in a footnote, because a
          hedge a reader has to go looking for is not a hedge.
        */}
        <span class="chip rt__eg">illustrative</span>
      </p>

      {/*
        ── THE REFUSED FIGURE, PUT BACK INTO THE LEDGER SO IT CAN BE TAKEN OUT

        WHAT THIS ADDS AND WHAT IT REFUSED TO ADD. The section already states
        that one figure was dropped and draws the drop, hung under Ground, in
        the refusal colour, named. What a reader could not do until now is see
        what the ledger looks like WITH the ungrounded claim in it, which is the
        state every competing tool ships as its finished answer. This is that
        state, one press away, with the claim struck through.

        NO FIGURE WAS INVENTED FOR IT, AND THAT WAS THE DESIGN CONSTRAINT RATHER
        THAN A LIMITATION OF IT. The obvious build of this control gives the
        refused claim a number, adds it to the total, and lets the reader watch
        a bigger total shrink back. That number would have to be made up, on the
        one section of the site whose entire subject is a machine refusing to
        make a number up, in a file whose header records that the source concept
        was rejected for exactly this ("that version invents a notice number,
        two PDF filenames, a citation reference and an AUDIT HASH ... inventing
        evidence is the precise failure this whole section is built to
        demonstrate the engine refusing"). There is no value behind `local
        supply chain spend`. That is what "no recorded commitment" means.

        SO THE TOTAL DOES NOT MOVE, AND THAT IS THE STRONGER DEMONSTRATION. A
        reader presses the control, the claim appears in the ledger struck
        through, and £108,000 sits exactly where it was. An ungrounded claim
        adds nothing to an answer because there was never anything behind it to
        add. A number ticking up and back down would have said the opposite: that
        the refused figure was worth something the engine chose not to count.

        CORRECT AND COMPLETE WITH NO JAVASCRIPT. This row is rendered VISIBLE
        and the control below it is rendered `hidden`, so a reader with no
        script gets the fuller ledger and no button that does nothing. The
        script's first act is to reveal the control and collapse the row, which
        is the identical contract `.wt__pause` and `.in__pause` already state on
        this same page and for the same reason.
      */}
      <p class="rt__refused" id="rt-refused">
        <s class="rt__refused-claim">local supply chain spend</s>
        <span class="rt__refused-why">
          No recorded commitment, so it carries no figure. The total above is the
          same with it as without it.
        </span>
      </p>

      {/*
        `control chip` is the site's existing recipe for a chip that is also a
        target, already worn by `.art__pill` in layouts/Article.astro and by the
        four glossary filters. Between styles/controls.css and styles/labels.css
        it arrives with its fill, edge, ink, cursor, response, face, size,
        weight, tracking, padding, pill radius and 36px target. This file states
        the gap above it and nothing else.

        `aria-expanded` and `aria-controls` rather than a live region: this is a
        disclosure over content that is already in the document, and the row it
        controls sits directly above it, so a reader who toggles it lands on the
        change without being read to.
      */}
      <button
        class="control chip rt__toggle"
        type="button"
        data-rt-toggle
        aria-expanded="true"
        aria-controls="rt-refused"
        hidden
      >Take the refused figure out</button>
    </figure>

    {/*
      THE SECOND HALF OF THE CORE PRINCIPLE, AND IT CLOSES THE DRAWING RATHER
      THAN INTRODUCING IT. The concept prints this under its ledger and it
      belongs in the same place here: the reader has just watched a figure be
      removed, and this says what happens next and who is responsible for it.

      IT IS NOT THE HERO'S LINE. HeroStack closes with "The engine cannot clear
      its own gate. A named person approves.". The same fact, stated as a limit
      on the engine, eight sections earlier and before any of this has been
      shown. This states the CONSEQUENCE: the claim is gone before a human ever
      reads the draft, so the person signing is signing something the engine has
      already emptied of what it could not support. Both are worth having and
      neither is the other's transcript. If a future pass cuts one for
      repetition, it is this one that is load-bearing here.

      A PLAIN PARAGRAPH RATHER THAN `.meta`, and the hero's line is `.meta`, so
      the difference is worth a sentence. `.meta` is uppercase mono at --t-micro
      and is the site's recipe for PROVENANCE: a date, a reference, a stamp. The
      hero's eleven words fit that register. These twenty-two do not: they are
      two sentences of prose making an argument, and set in uppercase mono they
      would read as shouting rather than as a conclusion. It takes the base `p`
      size and the body leading, so it introduces no recipe of any kind.
    */}
    <p class="rt__signoff">
      The untraced claim is removed before a named approver reads the draft. A person signs off, not
      the software.
    </p>

    {/*
      TWO LINKS, AND THE SECOND IS NOT OPTIONAL. One is the product depth this
      section deliberately no longer explains in prose. The other is the standing
      rule for the whole page: every figure still traces to something, and
      /sources is where it traces to. The £27,000 above is listed there as an
      illustrative placeholder rather than a National TOMs proxy value, which is
      the strongest form of that statement this site has ever published.
    */}
    <p class="rt__cta">
      <a href="/crowmark/">How the drafter works</a>
      <a href="/sources/#homepage">Sources</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</Section>

<script>
  /**
   * Enhancement only. The document is already correct and complete before this
   * runs: every step is visible and the sum reads £108,000.
   *
   * What it adds is a count-up towards the value ALREADY IN THE DOM, parsed from
   * the element rather than hard-coded here, so the number can never disagree
   * with the markup. If this script is blocked, throws, or never runs, the
   * section is unchanged.
   */
  /**
   * The refused-figure toggle. Also enhancement only, and it runs BEFORE the
   * reduced-motion return below on purpose: a disclosure is not motion, and a
   * reader who has asked for stillness should still get the control.
   *
   * The row it governs is rendered visible and this button is rendered `hidden`,
   * so with no script the ledger carries the refused claim and there is no dead
   * button beside it. The first two lines here invert that pair, which is the
   * only moment the two states can disagree.
   */
  /**
   * The scrubber. Returns the function that moves the playhead, so the refusal
   * control below can drive it, or null when the markup is not there.
   *
   * THE STATE IS THREE WORDS ON FIVE ELEMENTS AND NOTHING ELSE. `done`, `now`
   * and `next` on each step, written here and read by the stylesheet. There is
   * no per-value selector to write out five times, no class to keep in step
   * with an attribute, and no numeric state anywhere but the input's own value,
   * which is the control the reader is holding.
   */
  function initScrub(root: HTMLElement) {
    const wrap = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-rt-scrub]');
    const range = root.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>('[data-rt-range]');
    const steps = Array.from(root.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.rt__step'));
    /* The control describes the rail, so a mismatch means it would be lying
       about something. Leave it hidden rather than ship a slider whose ends are
       not the ends of the sequence. */
    if (!wrap || !range || !steps.length || steps.length !== Number(range.max)) return null;

    const paint = (n: number) => {
      steps.forEach((li, i) => {
        li.dataset.rtState = i + 1 < n ? 'done' : i + 1 === n ? 'now' : 'next';
      });
      /* A bare "3" is what a range announces on its own, which says nothing
         about a trace. The step's own heading already carries its number and
         its verb, so it is read rather than restated. */
      const name = steps[n - 1].querySelector('.rt__name')?.textContent ?? '';
      range.setAttribute('aria-valuetext', name.replace(/\s+/g, ' ').trim());
    };

    wrap.hidden = false;
    range.addEventListener('input', () => paint(Number(range.value)));

    return (n: number) => {
      range.value = String(n);
      paint(n);
    };
  }

  function initRefusedToggle(root: HTMLElement, goTo: ((n: number) => void) | null) {
    const btn = root.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-rt-toggle]');
    const row = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>('#rt-refused');
    if (!btn || !row) return;

    /*
     * ── THE TOGGLE IS THE REFUSAL TRIGGER, AND IT ALWAYS WAS ────────────────
     *
     * Owner: a control that shows an untraced figure being rejected. This
     * button already performs exactly that transaction on the LEDGER, in
     * words: pressing "Take the refused figure out" removes the ungrounded
     * claim and leaves the total where it was. What it did not do was connect
     * that to the RAIL, where the refusal is drawn and where it actually
     * happens. Now it does: the playhead lands on the step the drop hangs from
     * and the drop replays, so the two halves of one event read as one event.
     *
     * THE STEP IS FOUND, NOT WRITTEN DOWN. `DROP_AT` in the frontmatter is the
     * one place the section decides where grounding fails, and the drop box is
     * rendered inside that step's own list item. Reading the position back off
     * the markup means this cannot name a different stage from the one the
     * drawing hangs the refusal under.
     */
    const dropAt = Array.from(root.querySelectorAll('.rt__step')).findIndex((li) =>
      li.querySelector('.rt__drop'),
    ) + 1;

    /* THE LABEL IS THE BUTTON'S OWN TEXT NODE AND NOT A <span> INSIDE IT.
       A wrapping span inherits the chip's mono uppercase type without the chip's
       box, and scripts/check-treatments.js reads that as a FIFTH micro-label
       recipe: it measured this exact element on the first build of this control,
       reporting "JetBrains Mono 13.00px lh16.25 track1.04 w500 uppercase no box"
       on one route. There are four label recipes on this site and there is no
       fifth. Writing the button's textContent keeps the label inside the boxed
       chip that already exists. */
    btn.hidden = false;
    let shown = false;
    /* `hidden` rather than a class, so the row is out of the accessibility tree
       as well as out of the layout and cannot be read while it is not on screen. */
    row.hidden = true;
    btn.setAttribute('aria-expanded', 'false');
    btn.textContent = 'Put the refused figure back';

    btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
      shown = !shown;
      row.hidden = !shown;
      btn.setAttribute('aria-expanded', String(shown));
      btn.textContent = shown ? 'Take the refused figure out' : 'Put the refused figure back';
      /* Taking it out IS the refusal, so that is the press that moves the rail.
         Putting it back is the reader asking to see the other tool's answer,
         which is a state rather than an act, and it moves nothing. */
      if (!shown && goTo && dropAt > 0) goTo(dropAt);
    });
  }

  function initReasoningTrace() {
    const root = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-rt]');
    if (!root) return;

    /* Both before the reduced-motion return, and for the same reason the
       toggle already was: neither is motion. A scrubber is a reader moving
       through content at their own speed, which is the opposite of the thing
       that preference asks to stop, and the stylesheet takes the animation off
       every state it sets. */
    const goTo = initScrub(root);
    initRefusedToggle(root, goTo);

    const reduced = window.matchMedia?.('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches;
    if (reduced || typeof IntersectionObserver !== 'function') return;

    const sumEl = root.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-rt-sum]');
    if (!sumEl) return;

    /* READ AS DATA, NEVER PARSED BACK OUT OF THE TEXT. See the note above
       SUM_TOTAL in the frontmatter for the defect this replaces. */
    const target = Number(sumEl.dataset.rtTotal);
    if (!Number.isFinite(target) || target <= 0) return;

    /* THE SAME FORMATTER THE TEMPLATE USED, so the frames, the final value and
       the server-rendered string are one function of one number and cannot
       differ by a separator, a symbol or a locale. */
    const fmt = new Intl.NumberFormat('en-GB', {
      style: 'currency',
      currency: 'GBP',
      maximumFractionDigits: 0,
    });

    let played = false;
    const io = new IntersectionObserver(
      (entries) => {
        for (const e of entries) {
          if (!e.isIntersecting || played) continue;
          played = true;
          io.disconnect();

          const startedAt = performance.now();
          const DURATION = 1400;
          const easeOutQuart = (t: number) => 1 - Math.pow(1 - t, 4);

          const frame = (now: number) => {
            const t = Math.min(1, Math.max(0, (now - startedAt) / DURATION));
            sumEl.textContent = fmt.format(Math.round(target * easeOutQuart(t)));
            if (t < 1) requestAnimationFrame(frame);
            // The last frame is the target formatted by the same function that
            // wrote the markup, so there is no authored string to restore and
            // no formatting difference that could survive the animation.
            else sumEl.textContent = fmt.format(target);
          };
          requestAnimationFrame(frame);
        }
      },
      { threshold: 0.15 }
    );

    io.observe(root);
  }

  initReasoningTrace();
</script>

<style>
  /* ── The ambient rig ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Identical in every value to the five sections that already ship it. The
     wrapper isolates so a screen-blended field can never climb over the copy,
     and the bleed is exactly one gutter: enough to reach the viewport edge
     rather than drawing a seam one gutter in from it, and never one pixel
     more, because the sitewide gate fails any page whose scrollWidth exceeds
     its clientWidth at 390, 768 or 1440. A percentage bleed cannot do this;
     see MarketShape.astro for the measured failure. */
  .rt {
    position: relative;
    isolation: isolate;
    /* ONE CURVE, NAMED, BECAUSE IT WAS ALREADY WRITTEN TWICE. The drop box and
       the tick that hangs it both settle with a slight overshoot, and both
       carried the same literal cubic-bezier. The scrub below is a third reader
       of it, and three copies of a design value in one file is the shape RULE
       0-C exists to stop. It is local because tokens.css has no overshoot
       easing to reach for and that file is not this change's to edit: if a
       second component ever wants a settle, it graduates there rather than
       being copied here. */
    --rt-settle: cubic-bezier(0.34, 1.56, 0.64, 1);
  }

  .rt__amb {
    position: absolute;
    /* Scroll-driven parallax throw. See styles/tokens.css. */
    --par: 42px;
    inset-block: -16%;
    inset-inline: calc(-1 * var(--gutter));
    z-index: -1;
    overflow: hidden;
    pointer-events: none;
    /* All four edges are masked. `overflow: hidden` stops the bleed widening
       the page but cuts a 150px blur off square, and a blurred field ending in
       a straight line draws a visible lighter rectangle. One radial mask fades
       every edge at once, so there is no axis for it to miss. */
    /*
     * `closest-side`, NOT `115% 105%`, AND THAT IS A MEASURED CORRECTION.
     *
     * The earlier value was chosen to fade all four edges at once, which it
     * did, but only partly. An ellipse of 115% x 105% has semi-axes of 0.575w
     * and 0.525h, so the middle of the left edge sits at r = 0.5 / 0.575 =
     * 0.87 of the way along a ramp that only reaches transparent at 1.0. About
     * 25% of the field survives there. On a 150px blur whose own falloff is
     * nearly finished, 25% of nearly nothing is nothing, which is why it held.
     *
     * specs/ULTRA-PREMIUM-GAP.md §3 raises the orb stops by roughly 1.5x to
     * 1.9x, and 25% of that is a visible vertical line. Measured on the built
     * result at 1440: a lighter RECTANGLE across this section and the four
     * others sharing this recipe, one gutter in from each side, exactly the
     * defect the original note describes and thought it had closed.
     *
     * `closest-side` gives semi-axes of 0.5w and 0.5h, so the ramp reaches
     * transparent EXACTLY at the middle of every edge and past it at every
     * corner. There is no arithmetic left to get wrong, and it stays correct
     * when a section changes shape.
     */
    -webkit-mask-image: radial-gradient(
      closest-side,
      rgb(0 0 0) 42%,
      transparent 100%
    );
    mask-image: radial-gradient(closest-side, rgb(0 0 0) 42%, transparent 100%);
  }

  .rt__amb b {
    position: absolute;
    border-radius: 50%;
    filter: blur(150px);
    mix-blend-mode: screen;
  }

  /* ONE FIELD, IN VIOLET, AND THIS SECTION HAD THE MOST TO LOSE BY KEEPING
     TWO. The low-right field's note already made half the argument ("this is
     the section where orchid means refused, so the falloff takes violet and
     the drawn cross keeps orchid to itself"), and stopped one hue short. This
     is also the section where the tick means VERIFIED, and a 560px teal light
     across its whole top edge is the same dilution one step to the left.

     So the surviving field takes the high-left geometry and the violet the
     other field was already carrying for exactly this reason. Every mark in
     the trace keeps its own colour and is now the only saturated thing in the
     frame, which is what A-113 asked for. The teal has gone from the light and
     from nowhere else. */
  .rt__amb-a {
    width: 560px;
    height: 420px;
    left: -6%;
    top: -20%;
    background: var(--orb-violet);
    opacity: 0.45;
  }

  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .rt__amb b { filter: blur(90px); width: 320px; height: 280px; }
  }

  /* ── The brief ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     PADDING ONLY. The border, the radius, the fill, the hairline and the shadow
     ladder all come from `sv-raised` in styles/surfaces.css, which replaced the
     55%-alpha card here. Restating any of them would put this file back among
     the twenty-one card recipes the audit found, and would also lose: an
     unlayered scoped rule beats the shared one, so a restated `background`
     would silently cancel the opaque step. */
  .rt__brief {
    padding: var(--blk-pad);
  }

  /* NO COLOUR, AND THAT IS A CORRECTION RATHER THAN A TIDY-UP, 2026-08-05.
     This said `color: var(--c-ambient-start)` and it PAINTED NOTHING: the
     eyebrow's clipped spectrum gradient carried `-webkit-text-fill-color:
     transparent`, so the declaration resolved and was never seen. Deleting the
     gradient sitewide would have made it visible for the first time. A
     decorative "Worked example" label rendered in the pigment that means
     verified, on the homepage, introduced by a change whose whole purpose is to
     stop exactly that. The label now inherits the one eyebrow colour from
     styles/labels.css, which is what every other eyebrow on this page takes. */
  .rt__brief-label {
    margin: 0 0 var(--stack-1);
  }

  /* The card holds a label and a question now, so the question no longer needs
     a bottom margin separating it from a criteria table that is not there. The
     award-weighting rules are deleted with their markup rather than left
     orphaned: a recipe nothing renders is how a removed block quietly grows
     back. */
  /*
   * ── THE 68ch CAP IS GONE, AND IT IS THE WIDTH HALF OF A-104 ITEM 8 ────────
   *
   * Owner, 2026-08-05, on this section: the example must be wider, and the
   * diagram style is approved and must not change. The diagram is the five-step
   * rail below and not one pixel of it is touched. The only thing in this
   * section that was narrower than the panel holding it was this measure: the
   * brief panel runs the full container and the question ran 68ch inside it,
   * centred, with the rest of the panel empty on both sides.
   *
   * A HEADING-TIER SENTENCE TAKES THE MEASURE; A PARAGRAPH DOES NOT. That is
   * already the site's rule rather than a new one. `.section__title` runs the
   * full head block at --t-h2, and the two things in this section that stay
   * capped are the standfirst and `.rt__signoff`, both of which are prose. This
   * is one sentence set at --t-h4 in the display face: it is the artefact being
   * quoted, and a quoted demand reads as a demand at the width of the box it is
   * quoted in.
   *
   * `margin: 0 auto` STAYS, AND IT IS NOT NOW REDUNDANT. `.sv-raised:not(figure)`
   * in styles/surfaces.css centres the words in this panel, and the auto margins
   * are what centre the BOX. With no cap they resolve to zero today; they are
   * what stops the question hugging the panel's left edge the moment anybody
   * puts a cap back, which is the defect this pairing was written for.
   */
  .rt__q {
    margin: 0 auto;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--t-h4);
    font-weight: 700;
    line-height: 1.4;
    color: var(--c-text);
  }

  /* The provenance line. Ink and rhythm only: `.meta` carries the mono face,
     the tracking, the uppercase and --t-micro, so this introduces no recipe.
     --c-text-muted is the quietest ink on the ladder, which is right for a line
     that identifies a document rather than making a claim. */
  .rt__brief-src {
    margin: var(--stack-1) 0 0;
    color: var(--c-text-muted);
  }

  /* ── The five-step rail ────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  /* The raised panel. It holds the outer spacing and nothing else; every pixel
     of the rail's geometry stays on the list below, so the split changes which
     element is which and changes no measurement. `figure.sv-raised` has its
     user-agent margin zeroed in styles/surfaces.css. */
  .rt__railpanel { margin: var(--sec-gap) 0 0; }

  .rt__rail {
    margin: 0;
    padding: var(--blk-pad) 0;
    list-style: none;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(5, minmax(0, 1fr));
    /* Steps start at the top so the number, name and dot rows line up across
       all five columns; only step 02 grows downward, and it grows into space
       nothing else occupies. */
    align-items: start;
  }

  .rt__step {
    display: grid;
    /* FIVE ROWS SINCE 2026-08-07, not four: the log line sits between the name
       and the rail cell, so the rail still lands on one line across all five
       columns only if every column declares the same track count. */
    grid-template-rows: auto auto auto auto auto;
    justify-items: center;
    text-align: center;
    padding-inline: 6px;
  }

  /* ── THE RAIL WAS DRAWN AT THREE DIFFERENT HEIGHTS ────────────────────────
   *
   * MEASURED at 1440 on the built page: the rail segment under Retrieve sat at
   * y=369, the one under Ground at y=435 and the ones under Compute, Check and
   * Cite at y=403. Three heights across five columns, with two visible steps in
   * a line whose entire job is to be one continuous spine. The five list items
   * measured 204, 378, 224, 224 and 224px tall.
   *
   * THE CAUSE IS THE LOG LINE, AND THE COMMENT ABOVE IT NAMED A MECHANISM THAT
   * DOES NOT EXIST. `.rt__detail` is documented as reserving "the tallest case
   * so all five columns agree on where the rail sits", with a `min-height` doing
   * it. There is no `min-height` on that rule and there never was. What is
   * actually there is `text-wrap: balance` and a 22ch measure, which even out
   * the lines WITHIN a column and say nothing about the count ACROSS columns.
   * At 1440 the five details wrap to 3, 5, 4, 4 and 4 lines, so the rail landed
   * at three heights, exactly as those numbers predict. A comment asserting a
   * guard that is not in the code is worse than no comment: it is why nobody
   * looked.
   *
   * SUBGRID, WHICH IS THE ONLY CONSTRUCTION THAT ACTUALLY CLOSES IT. The five
   * steps become one shared set of five row tracks, so track 4 (the rail cell)
   * resolves to the same y in every column whatever the copy above it does, and
   * the drop box in track 5 deepens only track 5, which is BELOW the rail. That
   * is the property a min-height cannot give: a reserved height is a guess at
   * the tallest case at one width, and it is wrong the moment the copy or the
   * measure changes.
   *
   * WHY NOT BothSides.astro's CONSTRUCTION, since that section solved the same
   * problem without subgrid. Its rows are `1fr auto auto auto auto 1fr` and the
   * two flexible rows absorb the difference, which works because every one of
   * its stations holds the SAME elements. This rail does not: Ground carries a
   * drop box that no other step has, and it hangs BELOW the rail. Stretching all
   * five to Ground's height would push the other four rails down past it and
   * make the break worse rather than closing it.
   *
   * @supports, AND THE FALLBACK IS THE CURRENT BEHAVIOUR. Subgrid is Firefox 71,
   * Safari 16 and Chrome 117, so a browser without it keeps exactly the layout
   * that shipped yesterday: a rail with two small steps in it. Cosmetic, not
   * broken, and nothing else in the section depends on it.
   *
   * NOT APPLIED BELOW 860px, where the media query at the foot of this file
   * turns the rail 90 degrees, makes the list one column and takes the
   * horizontal segments out with `display: none`. There is no shared row to
   * align there and the steps are rows of the list rather than columns of it. */
  @supports (grid-template-rows: subgrid) {
    @media (min-width: 861px) {
      .rt__rail {
        grid-template-rows: auto auto auto auto auto;
        align-items: stretch;
      }

      .rt__step {
        grid-row: 1 / -1;
        grid-template-rows: subgrid;
      }
    }
  }

  /* ── THE LOG LINE ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   *
   * Mono, because this is the engine reporting rather than the page talking,
   * and mono is already what this section uses for its machine tags. Muted and
   * one tier down, so it reads under the verb rather than competing with it.
   *
   * A MEASURE IS SET AND IT IS THE REASON THE RAIL STILL WORKS. The five steps
   * are grid columns of equal width; a long line in one column would deepen
   * that column alone and drop the rail out of true. `text-wrap: balance` keeps
   * the two or three lines even, and the min-height reserves the tallest case
   * so all five columns agree on where the rail sits.
   */
  .rt__detail {
    margin: var(--stack-1) 0 0;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--t-micro);
    line-height: var(--lh-body);
    color: var(--c-text-muted);
    text-wrap: balance;
    max-inline-size: 22ch;
  }

  .rt__n {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--t-counter);
    /* 0.14em of tracking on an 11px label is what made it read as a machine
       tag. At 64px the same value opens "01" into two unrelated glyphs, so it
       comes down to the display tier's own letter-spacing. */
    line-height: 1;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  /*
   * SINGLE WORDS, AND THAT IS LOAD-BEARING. Retrieve, Ground, Compute, Check
   * and Cite cannot wrap, so the rows above the rail are the same height in
   * every column and the rail lands on one line with no subgrid and nothing to
   * measure. A two-word step name would break the geometry silently.
   *
   * Sized up to --t-h3 now the captions under them are gone: at --t-h4 a single
   * word above a lone dot read as a caption itself. The heading LEVEL is
   * unchanged. Still single words, so still unwrappable, so the rail still lands.
   */
  .rt__name {
    margin: 10px 0 0;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: var(--t-h3);
    font-weight: 700;
    line-height: 1.2;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    color: var(--c-text);
  }

  /* The rail, drawn per cell so five 100%-wide segments butt into one line. No
     absolute positioning, nothing to re-measure on resize or after webfonts. */
  .rt__railcell {
    position: relative;
    display: block;
    width: calc(100% + 12px);
    height: 22px;
    margin-top: var(--stack-1);
  }

  /*
   * 2px AND SPECULAR, NOT 1px AND FLAT. Figma `214:92`, and the same reasoning
   * the craft table gives for every other edge on this site: a flat 1px line of
   * one colour is a drawn rule, and a two-stop line with a highlight along it is
   * an edge catching a light. The two-colour glow underneath (a tight teal
   * core under a wide violet halo) is the same construction the hero planes and
   * the integration chips already carry.
   *
   * THE HUE IS UNCHANGED AND THE RAIL IS STILL NOT A MARKER. Teal at 45% was
   * the rest value and teal is still what the rail is made of; what is added is
   * white in the middle of its own thickness and light underneath it. The dots
   * ON the rail are the markers and they are untouched.
   */
  .rt__railcell::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset-inline: 0;
    top: 50%;
    margin-top: -1px;
    height: 2px;
    /* --grad-ramp across the rail's own 2px thickness: white on top, teal
       underneath, which is what puts light under the rail rather than colour
       on it. */
    --ramp-from: color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-text) 30%, transparent);
    --ramp-to: color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-verified) 55%, transparent);
      /* Written here, not var(--grad-ramp). A custom property's var()s are
         substituted where it is DECLARED, so a knob token on :root bakes in its
         fallbacks and inherits finished. Measured: --grad-ramp computes to
         `linear-gradient(180deg, #161B2F, #0E1220 100%)` on every element,
         whatever knobs it sets. It hid every blog hero photo behind an opaque
         rectangle before this was found. Colours still come from tokens. */
    background: linear-gradient(
      180deg,
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-text) 30%, transparent),
      color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-verified) 55%, transparent) 100%
    );
    box-shadow:
      0 0 6px color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-verified) 30%, transparent),
      0 0 18px color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-ambient-end) 18%, transparent);
  }

  /* --grad-fade at the two outer ends. 45% -> the token's 55%: the identical
     rail end two sections up ran at 55%, and the two could not see each other. */
  .rt__step:first-child .rt__railcell::before {
    background: var(--grad-fade);
  }

  .rt__step:last-child .rt__railcell::before {
    --fade-angle: 270deg;
    background: var(--grad-fade);
  }

  /* ══ M5 · THE REFUSAL ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   *
   * Figma `147:2`, "If one thing on this page animates, it is the drop at
   * Ground". TRIGGER IntersectionObserver, threshold 0.15.
   * CADENCE 5 beats, one per step, on the shared --m-beat.
   * PLAYS: everything fires ONCE. REDUCED MOTION final state.
   *
   * ── THE SPLIT THIS BLOCK USED TO DESCRIBE IS CLOSED, 2026-08-05 ───────────
   *
   * It read: "PLAYS: the light LOOPS. The refusal does not", and it called that
   * split the whole editorial decision in this file. The argument was that the
   * travelling light and the dots carry nothing, so they may repeat, while the
   * drop at Ground and the glow behind the Refused tally are the section's
   * ARGUMENT, and an argument re-staged every twelve seconds becomes a
   * performance.
   *
   * HALF OF THAT SURVIVES AND IT IS THE HALF THAT MATTERED. The refusal still
   * fires once and may never be made to loop. What has gone is the licence on
   * the other side: A-104 (owner, 2026-08-05) asks the homepage to stop moving
   * at readers, and A-111 measured this section at TWELVE perpetual animations,
   * more than any other, of which ten were the five beams and the five dots.
   * "Light carries nothing, so it may repeat" was a reason it was ALLOWED to
   * repeat, never a reason it had to.
   *
   * NOT ONE DURATION OR DELAY CHANGED. Each beam is still one beat with its
   * 40%-to-80% hold on the dot, the dots still bloom 0.4 of a beat behind their
   * own step, the drop still lands at 1.4 beats and the Refused glow at five.
   * The whole gesture runs exactly as it did and then stops instead of waiting
   * fifteen beats to start again.
   *
   * A reader who looks away misses nothing either way: every word, the cross,
   * the box and the tally are in the document at full contrast from first
   * paint, and the state the page holds afterwards is the state it shipped in
   * before any of this existed.
   *
   * IT WAS 300ms PER STEP AND IT IS NOW ONE BEAT, 600ms. That is the single
   * biggest retiming on the page and it is the one the grammar exists for: at
   * 300ms this rail's light moved at roughly twice the speed of the light on
   * the both-sides rail directly above it, on the same page, in the same
   * treatment, for no reason other than that the two files were written
   * separately. One beat is one station everywhere now.
   *
   * THIS SECTION HAS ALREADY FAILED THE AXE GATE ONCE, on colour-contrast
   * across 7 nodes, because a staged reveal here left elements semi-transparent
   * while the checker sampled them mid-transition. Everything below is shaped
   * by that:
   *
   *   - Not one property that affects the contrast of any text is animated.
   *     No opacity on a word, no colour on a word, no background under a word.
   *   - THE DROP AT GROUND IS DISPLACEMENT, NEVER OPACITY. The obvious design
   *     fades the box to about 0.7 as it "drops". That is the exact defect:
   *     semi-transparent text measures against nothing. The box TRANSLATES 8px
   *     and the tick grows to follow it, while every colour and every contrast
   *     ratio inside it is fixed from first paint to last frame. If the script
   *     dies half way, the worst outcome on the page is a box sitting 8px high.
   *     Cosmetic. Not a content defect.
   *   - Nothing is revealed. The five steps, the rail, the cross, the box, the
   *     words "dropped here" and the tally are all present and at full contrast
   *     before any of this exists.
   *
   * THE ORCHID FORK IS A MARKER AND IS DRAWN AS ONE. The light travelling the
   * rail is white-cored with a teal bloom. The trace that descends from Ground
   * into the refusal is a FLAT ORCHID STROKE with no white core, because that
   * is how a reader tells a lamp from a label, and this is the section the
   * whole page's colour vocabulary is spent on.
   */
  /*
   * THE TRACK EXISTS TO CLIP. The beam is a full-cell-width element
   * translating from -50% to +50%, so it sits half a column outside its cell
   * at each end. Unclipped, that grows the page's scrollWidth for as long as
   * the light is travelling: a horizontal-overflow failure that only exists
   * mid-animation, which is exactly the kind that a settled-page check misses.
   *
   * It is NOT the railcell itself. The railcell holds .rt__dot, whose two-radius
   * teal halo is a box-shadow, and clipping the railcell would cut that off.
   */
  .rt__track {
    position: absolute;
    inset-inline: 0;
    top: 50%;
    height: 18px;
    margin-top: -9px;
    overflow: hidden;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .rt__beam {
    display: none;
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    /* Full cell width with the band painted in the middle of it, so the travel
       is one transform and the band's size scales with the column instead of
       being a fixed px that reads wide on a phone. translateX(-50%) puts the
       band on the cell's left edge and 0 puts it exactly on the dot. */
    .rt__beam {
      display: block;
      position: absolute;
      top: 9px;
      inset-inline: 0;
      height: 1px;
      /* White core, teal bloom: --grad-beam at its default width and hue. The
         bloom stop sat at 43/57 here and 44/56 in three other sections, which
         is one recipe with two opinions; the token holds 44/56. */
      background: var(--grad-beam);
      box-shadow: 0 0 10px var(--c-ambient-glow);
      opacity: 0;
    }

    /* One beat per step: 40% of it travelling to the dot, then a hold ON the
       dot to 80%, which is what makes five steps read as five decisions rather
       than as one sweep. Five steps, five beats, and then it is over.

       IT WAS `var(--m-cycle) ... infinite`, five beats of travel and fifteen
       of nothing, repeating for as long as the tab was open. A-111 measured
       TWELVE perpetual animations in this section, the highest of any on the
       page, and ten of them were this rule and the dot bloom below it, once per
       step. Both are one-shots now, on the same `data-lit` reveal that already
       fires the drop at Ground. The duration is one beat because one beat is
       what a beam is visible for; see the keyframes for the arithmetic, which
       is unchanged to the millisecond. */
    [data-lit='on'] .rt__beam {
      animation: rt-beam var(--m-beat) var(--m-ease-travel) 1;
    }

    [data-lit='on'] .rt__step:nth-child(2) .rt__beam { animation-delay: var(--m-beat); }
    [data-lit='on'] .rt__step:nth-child(3) .rt__beam { animation-delay: calc(var(--m-beat) * 2); }
    [data-lit='on'] .rt__step:nth-child(4) .rt__beam { animation-delay: calc(var(--m-beat) * 3); }
    [data-lit='on'] .rt__step:nth-child(5) .rt__beam { animation-delay: calc(var(--m-beat) * 4); }

    /* The dot blooms as the light lands on it. SPREAD AND SCALE ONLY. The
       board specifies opacity 0.55 -> 1.0 -> 0.7, which would leave the dot
       dimmer at the end than the rest state it started from and breaks binding
       rule 02 on its own board. The dot stays at opacity 1 throughout and gets
       bigger and brighter instead. */
    [data-lit='on'] .rt__dot {
      animation: rt-dot var(--m-beat) var(--m-ease-land) 1;
    }

    /* 0.4 of a beat behind the step's own start, which is where rt-beam puts
       the light on the dot. Written as a fraction of the beat rather than as a
       millisecond total so it cannot come adrift from the travel above. */
    [data-lit='on'] .rt__step:nth-child(1) .rt__dot { animation-delay: calc(var(--m-beat) * 0.4); }
    [data-lit='on'] .rt__step:nth-child(2) .rt__dot { animation-delay: calc(var(--m-beat) * 1.4); }
    [data-lit='on'] .rt__step:nth-child(3) .rt__dot { animation-delay: calc(var(--m-beat) * 2.4); }
    [data-lit='on'] .rt__step:nth-child(4) .rt__dot { animation-delay: calc(var(--m-beat) * 3.4); }
    [data-lit='on'] .rt__step:nth-child(5) .rt__dot { animation-delay: calc(var(--m-beat) * 4.4); }

    /* ── AND HERE THE SECTION STOPS LOOPING ──────────────────────────────────
       The four rules below are the refusal, and every one of them runs exactly
       once. See the header note: light repeats, an argument does not. All four
       keep their original one-shot form and only their timing was moved onto
       the beat.

       THE DROP. Displacement, with a slight overshoot, at the moment the light
       is on Ground, step 02, so 1.4 beats. `both` so the box holds the 8px
       start until its delay arrives rather than snapping down and dropping
       again. */
    [data-lit='on'] .rt__drop-box {
      animation: rt-drop 420ms var(--rt-settle) calc(var(--m-beat) * 1.4) both;
    }

    /* The tick GROWS TO FOLLOW THE BOX, from the top, so the two stay joined
       through the whole 8px. Scaling from the wrong origin detaches the line
       from the rail it hangs off, which is the only thing making the box read
       as hanging from Ground at all. */
    [data-lit='on'] .rt__drop-tick {
      animation: rt-tick 420ms var(--rt-settle) calc(var(--m-beat) * 1.4) both;
    }

    /* THE CROSS USED TO DRAW ITSELF IN HERE, in two 160ms strokes over a cross
     * that was already there, and it does not any more. Removed 2026-08-03 under
     * the owner's instruction to stop the animation on the homepage's images:
     * the mark is drawn inside <svg class="rt__x">, and a stroke assembling
     * itself in place is the picture being drawn rather than a light crossing
     * it. The overlay <path class="rt__x-draw"> existed only to carry that
     * animation and is gone with it; the cross beneath was always the refusal
     * mark and is unchanged.
     *
     * THE REST OF THIS SECTION IS UNTOUCHED. The beam, the dots, the tick, the
     * drop box and the closing glow are all HTML, none of them is inside a
     * drawing, and every one of them still runs.
     */

    /* THE CLOSING BEAT, tying the drop at Ground to the count at the bottom.
       A glow behind the figure, never the figure's own colour or opacity. It
       fires as the light leaves the fifth step, so five beats. */
    [data-lit='on'] .rt__tally-row--refused dd {
      animation: rt-refused 500ms var(--m-ease-land) calc(var(--m-beat) * 5) 1;
    }

    /* ── AND THE SCRUB RE-RUNS THEM, ONE STEP AT A TIME ─────────────────────
     *
     * The three rules below name the SAME keyframes, the same durations and
     * the same easings as the one-shots above. Not one new figure is drawn and
     * nothing new moves. What changes is who triggers them: `data-lit` fires
     * the whole sequence once when the section arrives, and these fire one
     * step of it whenever the reader puts the playhead on that step.
     *
     * THE ATTRIBUTE IS WHAT RESTARTS THEM, which is why this is three
     * declarations rather than a script that toggles a class and forces a
     * reflow. A step going `done -> now` gains an animation it did not have,
     * so the engine starts it; going `now -> done` takes it away again. Drag
     * back and forth and each step re-runs on arrival, every time, with no
     * state kept anywhere.
     *
     * `animation-delay: 0s` IS LOAD-BEARING. The rules above stagger each step
     * by its position in the reveal, up to four beats for the fifth. Inherited
     * here that would mean pressing on step five and waiting 2.4 seconds for
     * anything to happen. Under the playhead a step is the first beat, always.
     *
     * INSIDE `no-preference`, so a reader who has asked for stillness gets the
     * scrubber, the ink change, the halo and the tick, and no travelling light.
     * The control still works; it simply does not perform.
     */
    /* `.rt__rail` IS IN EVERY SELECTOR BELOW AND IT IS NOT DECORATION. The
       five staggered delays above are written as
       `[data-lit='on'] .rt__step:nth-child(N) .rt__beam`, which is four
       compounds. Without the ancestor these rules are three, so the stagger
       would have outranked the `0s` and pressing on the fifth step would have
       waited 2.4 seconds before anything moved. Matched in a browser before it
       was written down: this is a specificity fix, not a habit.

       THE DOT'S BLOOM IS DELIBERATELY NOT RE-RUN. `rt-dot` sets
       `translate(-50%, -50%)` in every one of its offsets, which is correct for
       the absolutely positioned dot of the wide layout and is a half-width
       shove on the STATIC dot the rail becomes under 861px. The playhead
       already opens that dot and widens its halo, without motion and at every
       width, so there is nothing here that re-running the keyframes would add. */
    .rt__rail .rt__step[data-rt-state='now'] .rt__beam {
      animation: rt-beam var(--m-beat) var(--m-ease-travel) 0s 1;
    }

    /* THE REFUSAL, WHERE THE REFUSAL HAPPENS. Only step 02 renders a drop box,
       so this rule fires on exactly one stage of the scrub, which is the whole
       argument of the section restated as a trigger. The button under the
       ledger drives the playhead here, so pressing "Take the refused figure
       out" plays the drop rather than only editing a list. */
    .rt__rail .rt__step[data-rt-state='now'] .rt__drop-box {
      animation: rt-drop 420ms var(--rt-settle) 0s both;
    }

    .rt__rail .rt__step[data-rt-state='now'] .rt__drop-tick {
      animation: rt-tick 420ms var(--rt-settle) 0s both;
    }
  }

  /* ONE BEAT PER STEP, AND THE BLOCK IS NOW THAT BEAT AND NOTHING ELSE. It was
     written against the twenty-beat cycle as 0.6/2/4/4.6/5% with the last
     fifteen sixteenths holding the beam parked off the far edge. Divide each of
     those by 5% and you get the offsets below: 12/40/80/92/100. Not one instant
     moved, and the hold ON the dot is still 40% to 80% of the beat, 240ms of
     travel and 360ms stationary.

     THE LAST OFFSET IS DELIBERATELY NOT THE RESTING STATE. The beam ends
     translated off the RIGHT edge at opacity 0: it crosses and leaves rather
     than resolving to where the element sits. That is what keeps
     scripts/check-motion.js rule 2 from reading a travelling light as an
     entrance belonging in motion.css, and it is why the 100% offset must never
     be "tidied" to translateX(0). */
  @keyframes rt-beam {
    0%    { transform: translateX(-50%); opacity: 0; }
    12%   { opacity: 1; }
    /* Arrival ON the dot at 40% of the beat, then a hold to 80%: 240ms of
       travel and 360ms stationary, which is the pause the board specifies and
       the thing that makes five steps read as five decisions. */
    40%   { transform: translateX(0); opacity: 1; }
    80%   { transform: translateX(0); opacity: 1; }
    92%   { opacity: 1; }
    100%  { transform: translateX(50%); opacity: 0; }
  }

  /* 260ms of bloom inside a one-beat block, which is where 1% and 2.2% of the
     old cycle sat: 20% and 44% of a beat. Opens and closes on scale(1) with the
     rest-state shadow, so the dot is byte-identical to its shipped state before
     the bloom and after it, and holds that state for good once the animation
     ends. The 44%, 100% pair is kept rather than collapsed to a single 100%
     offset: it is what states that the dot has SETTLED at 44% and is merely
     held thereafter, and it is also what keeps a non-identity translate on the
     block's final offset, which is what stops check-motion.js reading a
     centred, positioned dot as an element arriving. */
  @keyframes rt-dot {
    0%          { transform: translate(-50%, -50%) scale(1); }
    20%         {
      transform: translate(-50%, -50%) scale(1.3);
      box-shadow: 0 0 10px var(--c-verified-glow), 0 0 26px var(--c-verified-glow);
    }
    44%, 100%   { transform: translate(-50%, -50%) scale(1); }
  }

  /* 8px, and nothing else. No opacity, no colour, no background. */
  @keyframes rt-drop {
    from { transform: translateY(-8px); }
    to   { transform: translateY(0); }
  }

  @keyframes rt-tick {
    from { transform: scaleY(0.76); }
    to   { transform: scaleY(1); }
  }

  /* @keyframes rt-cross was declared here and went with the rule that used it. */

  @keyframes rt-refused {
    0%   { text-shadow: 0 0 12px color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-refused) 0%, transparent); }
    40%  { text-shadow: 0 0 12px color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-refused) 65%, transparent); }
    100% { text-shadow: 0 0 12px color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-refused) 0%, transparent); }
  }

  /* Teal = verified. Every step on this rail is one the engine completed. */
  .rt__dot {
    position: absolute;
    left: 50%;
    top: 50%;
    transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
    width: 10px;
    height: 10px;
    /* The tick the scrub lands in it is a grid item rather than a positioned
       pseudo-element, and that is what makes it survive the vertical layout
       below: `.rt__dot` is `position: absolute` here and `static` under 861px,
       so anything positioned against it would be positioned against something
       else down there. A centred grid child does not care which. */
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--c-verified);
    /* Two radii, not one: a tight core and a wide halo read as light, where a
       single blur reads as a smudge. */
    box-shadow: 0 0 6px var(--c-verified-glow), 0 0 22px var(--c-verified-glow);
  }

  /* ══ THE SCRUB STATES ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   *
   * Three words, written on the five list items by the script and read here.
   * NOT ONE OF THEM EXISTS UNTIL A READER MOVES THE CONTROL, so every selector
   * below matches nothing on the section as it rests, which is the condition
   * the owner's "the diagram is approved and must not change" puts on all of
   * this.
   *
   * AND NOT ONE OF THEM LOWERS THE CONTRAST OF A WORD. `next` has no rules at
   * all: a step ahead of the playhead is drawn exactly as it is drawn today,
   * rather than dimmed. `now` moves the log line from the quietest ink on the
   * ladder UP one tier. This section failed the axe gate once on
   * colour-contrast across seven nodes, sampled mid-transition, and the
   * direction of travel is the whole guard against a second time.
   *
   * THE TICK IS DRAWN FROM TWO BORDERS AND COSTS NO MARKUP. An inline SVG per
   * step would have been five more paths in the heaviest document on the site,
   * for a mark that is nine pixels of two straight lines. It scales from
   * nothing rather than fading in, because a scale is a mark being DRAWN and an
   * opacity is content that failed to appear: check-motion.js draws that line
   * in as many words, and this is on the drawn side of it. --dur collapses to
   * 0.01ms under `prefers-reduced-motion`, so the mark still lands, it simply
   * lands at once.
   */
  .rt__dot::after {
    content: '';
    inline-size: 4px;
    block-size: 8px;
    /* The ground colour, so the tick reads as cut out of the teal dot rather
       than laid on top of it. 15.9:1 against --c-verified. */
    border: 0 solid var(--c-bg);
    border-inline-end-width: 2px;
    border-block-end-width: 2px;
    /* A tick is a corner turned 45 degrees. The nudge re-centres the corner in
       the box after the rotation, which the rotation itself does not. */
    transform: translate(0, -1px) rotate(45deg) scale(0);
    transition: transform var(--dur) var(--rt-settle);
  }

  .rt__step[data-rt-state='done'] .rt__dot::after {
    transform: translate(0, -1px) rotate(45deg) scale(1);
  }

  /* The dot has to hold a mark now, so it opens from 10px. Both states, so the
     rail does not step up and back down as the playhead passes. */
  .rt__step[data-rt-state='done'] .rt__dot,
  .rt__step[data-rt-state='now'] .rt__dot {
    width: 18px;
    height: 18px;
  }

  /* The step under the playhead. Light and ink only: a wider halo on the dot,
     the same brightness lift on its own segment of rail that a pointer already
     gets, and the log line one tier up the ladder. */
  .rt__step[data-rt-state='now'] .rt__dot {
    box-shadow: 0 0 10px var(--c-verified-glow), 0 0 30px var(--c-verified-glow);
  }

  .rt__step[data-rt-state='now'] .rt__railcell::before {
    filter: brightness(1.7);
  }

  .rt__step[data-rt-state='now'] .rt__detail {
    color: var(--c-text-sub);
  }

  /* ── The dropped figure ────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  /*
   * HUNG UNDER GROUND, because grounding is where a figure fails: this is the
   * step that looks each candidate number up against what was actually
   * recorded. It sits inside step 02's own list item, so a screen reader meets
   * it immediately after "each figure looked up" rather than at the end of the
   * section, and the visual position falls out of the grid for free.
   */
  .rt__drop {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    /* Wider than its column. The box has to read as a callout hanging off the
       rail, not as a fourth line of step 02, and the figure's name must not
       wrap to three lines inside it. */
    width: max-content;
    max-width: 30ch;
  }

  .rt__drop-tick {
    width: 1px;
    height: 34px;
    /* --grad-fade, fading in downwards. The HUE is the meaning and is the one
       thing this tick states for itself: orchid = refused, sitewide. The 60% it
       used to carry was not a decision, it was a third alpha for a 1px tick. */
    --fade-angle: 180deg;
    --fade-hue: var(--c-refused);
    background: var(--grad-fade);
    /* From the TOP, so when the tick grows to follow the dropping box it stays
       joined to the rail it hangs off. Scaling from the centre would detach it
       from Ground, which is the only thing making the box read as hanging from
       that step rather than floating under the diagram. */
    transform-origin: top;
  }

  /*
   * ORCHID = REFUSED, and it is never the only signal. The box carries the word
   * "dropped", a drawn cross, and the reason the figure failed. Remove all
   * colour and the refusal still reads, WCAG 1.4.1.
   */
  .rt__drop-box {
    margin: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: 0.35rem 0.6rem;
    padding: 0.6rem 0.9rem;
    border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-refused) 55%, transparent);
    border-radius: var(--radius-chip);
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-refused) 10%, transparent);
    /* THE REFUSAL GETS ITS OWN LIGHT, and it is a STATIC box-shadow rather than
       an animation. Figma `214:92` gives the drop box an orchid glow; the
       standing rule on this page is that light repeats and an argument does
       not, so this is light the box simply has, at every frame, from first
       paint, with no observer and no cycle. The one-shot glow on the Refused
       tally still fires exactly once; the cross no longer draws itself in at
       all, since it is inside an SVG and was removed with the other image
       animations on 2026-08-03. */
    box-shadow:
      0 0 22px color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-refused) 22%, transparent),
      0 8px 24px color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-floor) 70%, transparent);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--t-micro);
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  .rt__x {
    flex: none;
    width: 12px;
    height: 12px;
    color: var(--c-refused);
  }

  .rt__drop-name { color: var(--c-refused); }

  .rt__drop-why { color: var(--c-text-muted); }

  .rt__drop-tag {
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-refused) 20%, transparent);
    color: var(--c-refused);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  /* ── The scrubber ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   *
   * A figcaption on the drawing, which is what it is: the control that says
   * where in the trace you are looking. The panel's own vertical padding is on
   * the <ol>, so the caption states its own.
   *
   * `[hidden]` IS NOT RESTATED HERE, and that is the one difference from
   * `.tabsw-step` in TabSwitcher.astro. That rule needs it because it declares
   * a display value of its own that would outrank the attribute. This declares
   * `display: flex` too, so tokens.css's unlayered
   * `[hidden] { display: none !important }` is what carries it, and the
   * `!important` is exactly why one global rule replaced five local ones. */
  .rt__scrub {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--stack-1);
    padding: 0 var(--blk-pad) var(--blk-pad);
  }

  .rt__scrub-label {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--c-text-muted);
  }

  /*
   * THE NATIVE CONTROL, PAINTED WITH `accent-color` AND NOTHING ELSE. Rebuilding
   * a slider from `::-webkit-slider-thumb` and its three siblings would be a
   * fourth control recipe on a site that has spent a week getting to one, and it
   * would take the keyboard behaviour with it. `accent-color` moves the track
   * fill and the thumb onto the section's own verified teal in one declaration,
   * per engine, with the focus ring, the drag, the arrow keys and Home and End
   * left to the browser.
   *
   * --btn-h-sm IS THE TARGET, not the visual height. tokens.css names it as the
   * smallest a control on this site may ever be, and a range input's own box is
   * what a thumb has to find, so the box takes the floor and the drawn track
   * sits in the middle of it.
   *
   * --measure-prose, because it is a control over a five-column diagram and not
   * a full-width bar. The same 5/8 of the measure the standfirst and the
   * refusal sentence take, so it lines up with the prose either side of it.
   */
  .rt__scrub-range {
    inline-size: min(100%, var(--measure-prose));
    block-size: var(--btn-h-sm);
    accent-color: var(--c-verified);
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  .rt__scrub-range:focus-visible {
    outline: 3px solid var(--c-interactive);
    outline-offset: 3px;
  }

  /* ── The result ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  /* Padding and position only; see .rt__brief for why nothing else is here. */
  .rt__result {
    margin: var(--sec-gap) 0 0;
    padding: var(--blk-pad);
  }

  .rt__tally {
    margin: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(200px, 1fr));
    gap: var(--stack-1) var(--stack-2);
  }

  .rt__tally-row {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 12px;
    padding-bottom: 0.5rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--c-border);
  }

  .rt__tally-row dt {
    font-size: var(--t-body);
    color: var(--c-text-sub);
  }

  .rt__tally-row dd {
    transition: text-shadow var(--dur) var(--ease);
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    line-height: var(--lh-tight);
    font-size: var(--t-h4);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    color: var(--c-verified);
  }

  .rt__tally-row--refused dd { color: var(--c-refused); }

  .rt__tally-row--refused {
    border-bottom-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-refused) 40%, transparent);
  }

  .rt__sum {
    margin: var(--stack-2) 0 0;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--t-body);
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--c-text-sub);
    /* A formula has no spaces to break at. */
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  .rt__sum b {
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--c-text);
    /* Tabular figures so a counting number does not jitter its own width. */
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  /*
   * FLAGGED, NOT REFUSED. Orchid is the sitewide colour for both and never
   * appears decoratively; the refusal box adds a drawn cross and the word
   * "dropped", which this deliberately does not, so the two cannot be confused.
   * The word "illustrative" carries the meaning on its own with every pixel of
   * colour removed, which is the WCAG 1.4.1 test.
   */
  .rt__eg {
    border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-refused) 45%, transparent);
    white-space: nowrap;
    color: var(--c-refused);
  }

  /* ── THE REFUSED FIGURE IN THE LEDGER ─────────────────────────────────────
   *
   * A rule above the row rather than a box around it, so it reads as a line
   * struck OUT of the ledger and not as a second card. `.rt__drop-box` in the
   * rail is the boxed treatment and it is already the section's one refusal
   * object, so a second box down here would be the same drawing twice.
   *
   * `border-block-start` alone, never a `border` shorthand: three sides of
   * nothing is what a shorthand would assert, and the rule this draws is the
   * ledger's own division between the total and what did not make it in. */
  .rt__refused {
    margin: var(--stack-2) 0 0;
    padding-block-start: var(--stack-1);
    border-block-start: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-refused) 30%, transparent);
    font-size: var(--t-body);
    line-height: var(--lh-body);
    color: var(--c-text-sub);
  }

  /* <s>, so the strike is in the DOCUMENT and not painted on by CSS. A
   * text-decoration written here would leave the claim reading as a live figure
   * to anyone whose stylesheet did not load, on the one line of this page where
   * that would say the opposite of what is meant.
   *
   * THE HUE IS THE RESTING REFUSAL COLOUR, the same one `.rt__drop-name` two
   * hundred lines up already carries on this identical string, so the claim
   * looks the same in the ledger as it does in the rail. Mono, because it is a
   * ledger line item rather than prose, which is the same call `.rt__sum`
   * directly above makes. */
  .rt__refused-claim {
    display: block;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    color: var(--c-refused);
    /* A claim has no spaces to break at once it is struck through as one run. */
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  .rt__refused-why {
    display: block;
    margin-block-start: 6px;
    /* --measure-prose caps this at about 90 characters. It is a sentence of
       prose inside a drawn ledger, so it takes the prose measure rather than
       running the full width of a panel sized for a five-column rail. */
    max-width: var(--measure-prose);
    margin-inline: auto;
  }

  /* ONE DECLARATION, AND EVERYTHING ELSE IS A SHARED CLASS.
   *
   * `control chip` is the site's existing recipe for a chip that is also a
   * target, and it already ships on `.art__pill` and on the four `.gx-chip`
   * filters. styles/controls.css gives the fill, the edge, the ink, the cursor
   * and the pointer response. styles/labels.css gives the face, the size, the
   * weight, the tracking, the uppercase, the padding and the pill radius, and
   * its `:where(a, button, summary, [role='button']).chip` rule gives the 36px
   * min-height that makes it a target, which clears the 24px check-controls.js
   * rule 3 measures at 1440 and at 390.
   *
   * SO ONLY THE GAP ABOVE IT IS THIS FILE'S TO STATE. Anything else written
   * here would be an eleventh control recipe or a twelfth chip geometry, which
   * are the two counted defects those files exist to end, and the padding in
   * particular is part of the fingerprint check-treatments.js measures. */
  .rt__toggle {
    margin-block-start: var(--stack-2);
  }

  /* ── Close ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     The National TOMs foot is gone and its rule with it; the framework has its
     own entry on /sources. What closes the section is the principle, then two
     links. */

  /* Ink, rhythm and a measure. Nothing about the type: --t-body arrives from
     the base `p` rule in tokens.css and the leading from --lh-body, so this
     sentence is set exactly as every other sentence on the site is. The measure
     is the same 62ch a standfirst takes, because it is read the same way. */
  .rt__signoff {
    margin: var(--sec-gap) auto 0;
    max-width: 62ch;
    color: var(--c-text-sub);
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  .rt__cta {
    margin: var(--stack-2) 0 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    /* Centred on the same axis as the head block above it. A flex row defaults
       to flex-start, which left the pair sitting left under a centred heading. */
    justify-content: center;
    gap: 0 var(--stack-2);
  }

  .rt__cta a {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    min-height: 44px;
    color: var(--c-interactive);
    font-size: var(--t-body);
  }

  /* Thickness rather than colour, for the reason recorded on .prose a:hover in
     styles/prose.css: --c-interactive is white under V6, so a hover has nowhere
     brighter to go and a hue change would put a link on a marker. */
  .rt__cta a:hover { text-decoration-thickness: 2px; }

  .rt__cta a:focus-visible {
    outline: 3px solid var(--c-interactive);
    outline-offset: 3px;
    border-radius: 2px;
  }

  /* ══ THE STEP ANSWERS A CURSOR ═════════════════════════════════════════════
   *
   * Two :hover rules in this section before today, both on links.
   * specs/ULTRA-PREMIUM-GAP.md §2.6: 2.7% of this page's elements carry a
   * transition against 16.2% on the dark comparator, and that is the single
   * biggest lever it identifies.
   *
   * WHAT A STEP DOES UNDER THE POINTER: its counter comes up out of the ramp,
   * its name takes a soft glow behind it, its dot opens a wider halo, and its
   * segment of the rail brightens. Four things, one event, and every one of
   * them is light.
   *
   * TWO PROPERTIES ARE STILL UNAVAILABLE ON THE DOT FOR THE FIRST FEW SECONDS,
   * AND IT IS WORTH SAYING WHY, because the obvious hover was written first and
   * did nothing. `rt-dot` names both `transform` and `box-shadow` in its
   * keyframes, and a running animation owns a property outright for as long as
   * it runs, so a transition on either is invisible while it does. That USED TO
   * BE PERMANENT: this paragraph was written when `rt-dot` looped, and the
   * conflict therefore lasted the whole life of the page. As of 2026-08-05 it
   * is a one-shot, so the conflict lasts one beat from reveal and the hover is
   * unobstructed after that. The rule below is unchanged anyway, and
   * deliberately: `filter` is in neither keyframe block, so the dot's hover halo
   * is a drop-shadow that works during the bloom and after it. Same reason the
   * counter uses a filter. It is painted through a gradient clipped to the
   * glyph, and a text-shadow on a transparent fill draws nothing at all.
   *
   * THE REFUSAL IS NOT A HOVER TARGET. The drop box carries its light at every
   * frame from first paint. A refusal that only lights when a pointer finds it
   * would be a refusal a reader can miss, which is the one thing this section
   * exists to prevent.
   */
  .rt__n,
  .rt__name,
  .rt__dot,
  .rt__railcell::before,
  .rt__tally-row {
    transition:
      color var(--dur) var(--ease),
      border-color var(--dur) var(--ease),
      filter var(--dur) var(--ease),
      /* The dot opens from 10px to 18px as the playhead reaches it, so the two
         box properties join the list the four ink ones were already on. Both
         are animatable and neither is named by `rt-dot`, so the one-shot bloom
         cannot own them the way it owns transform and box-shadow. */
      width var(--dur) var(--ease),
      height var(--dur) var(--ease),
      text-shadow var(--dur) var(--ease);
  }

  @media (hover: hover) {
    .rt__step:hover .rt__n {
      filter: drop-shadow(0 2px 2px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.9))
        drop-shadow(0 0 24px color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-text) 34%, transparent));
    }

    .rt__step:hover .rt__name {
      text-shadow: 0 0 20px color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-text) 30%, transparent);
    }

    .rt__step:hover .rt__dot {
      filter: drop-shadow(0 0 12px var(--c-verified-glow));
    }

    .rt__step:hover .rt__railcell::before { filter: brightness(1.7); }

    /* The tally is a table of results, so its rows take the quietest response
       on the page: the hairline under the row brightens and the figure lifts.
       No background, because the row holds two text nodes and one of them is
       the Refused count. */
    .rt__tally-row:hover { border-bottom-color: var(--c-border-glow); }

    .rt__tally-row--refused:hover {
      border-bottom-color: color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-refused) 75%, transparent);
    }

    .rt__tally-row:hover dd {
      text-shadow: 0 0 16px color-mix(in srgb, currentColor 45%, transparent);
    }
  }

  /*
   * THE LINEAR FALLBACK, AND IT IS THE SHIPPED LAYOUT AT 390px. Five columns
   * across 390px is 66px each, which cannot hold the word "Compute" at --t-h4,
   * let alone a caption. Below 860px the rail turns 90 degrees: it runs down
   * the left as a single border, each step keeps its dot, and the dropped
   * figure stays attached to Ground where it belongs. The geometry changes;
   * every word survives, including the refusal.
   */
  @media (max-width: 860px) {
    .rt__rail {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: var(--stack-2);
      border-left: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-verified) 40%, transparent);
      padding-left: var(--stack-2);
    }

    .rt__step {
      position: relative;
      justify-items: start;
      text-align: left;
      padding-inline: 0;
    }

    .rt__railcell {
      /* Out of the flow and onto the vertical rail, level with its own step. */
      position: absolute;
      left: calc(-1 * var(--stack-2));
      top: 0;
      width: auto;
      height: auto;
      margin-top: 0;
      transform: translateX(-50%);
    }

    /* The horizontal segment has no neighbours to meet once the list is
       vertical, and left in place it draws a stub through the dot. */
    .rt__railcell::before { display: none; }

    .rt__dot {
      position: static;
      transform: none;
      /* `grid`, not `block`, and it changes nothing about the dot: it is what
         keeps the scrub's tick centred in it down here, where the dot is no
         longer positioned and a positioned mark would resolve against the
         railcell instead. Both values are block-level, so the row is
         unaffected. */
      display: grid;
    }

    /* The rail is vertical here and every step is a row, so a 64px counter
       above a 33px name would make the number the heading. It comes back to the
       tier above the name rather than to the 11px it was: --t-h4 keeps the
       ordering visible without taking the column. */
    .rt__n { font-size: var(--t-h4); letter-spacing: 0.06em; }

    .rt__name { margin-top: 2px; }

    /* ── AND THE 22ch MEASURE COMES OFF, 2026-08-29 ──────────────────────────
     *
     * `.rt__detail` is capped at 22ch up in the default block, and the reason
     * given there is the only reason it exists: the five steps are equal grid
     * COLUMNS, so one long line would deepen one column and drop the rail out
     * of true. This query is the point at which that stops being true. The rail
     * is vertical here and every step is a full-width row, so there are no
     * columns left to hold level and the cap is a desktop constraint standing
     * in a layout that does not have the problem.
     *
     * MEASURED AT 390: it wrapped "The published question / is read with its /
     * wording intact." into three short lines against a card 310px wide, so
     * every step body ran down the left half of its own row with the right half
     * empty. Nothing about the type changes, only how far it is allowed to run.
     */
    .rt__detail { max-inline-size: none; }

    .rt__drop {
      align-items: start;
      width: auto;
      max-width: 100%;
      margin-top: var(--stack-1);
    }

    /* A 34px stem into a left-aligned box points at nothing. */
    .rt__drop-tick { display: none; }

    .rt__drop-box { justify-content: flex-start; }
  }

</style>
